 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Precision:** The mapping relies on texture coordinates falling within $[0, 1]$. Precision errors at boundaries may require careful handling of tile indices [S1].
-*   **Mesh Complexity:** The replication area size is determined by the *largest* triangle. If the mesh contains triangles of vastly different sizes, this may lead to inefficient memory usage for smaller triangles [S1].
-*   **Boundary Conditions:** The tiling recipes must be large enough to cover the entire coordinate space. If the mesh extends beyond the pre-computed recipe bounds, the mapping will fail [S1].
+*   **Mesh Complexity:** The replication area size is determined by the *largest* triangle [S1]. If the mesh contains triangles of vastly different sizes, this may lead to inefficient memory usage for smaller triangles [S1].
+*   **Coordinate Coverage:** The tiling recipes must be large enough to cover the entire coordinate space ($[0, 1]$ for texture, full core grid for 3D) [S1].
+*   **Projection Details:** The exact method for determining which tiles are projected onto a specific triangle is not detailed in the sources and refers to the original Nanomatrix paper [S1].
 
